---
title: Chat Persistence
id: chat-persistence
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You want a conversation to outlive a single request: the transcript, whether\neach run finished or is still waiting on an interrupt, all still there after the\nprocess restarts. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":" is a chat middleware that writes that\nstate to a store you choose, so the server owns an authoritative copy of every\nthread."}]},{"type":"component","name":"tabs","attributes":{"variant":"package-manager","mode":"install"},"children":[],"properties":{"data-package-manager-meta":"{\"packagesByFramework\":{\"react\":[[\"@tanstack/ai-persistence\"]],\"vue\":[[\"@tanstack/ai-persistence\"]],\"solid\":[[\"@tanstack/ai-persistence\"]],\"svelte\":[[\"@tanstack/ai-persistence\"]],\"preact\":[[\"@tanstack/ai-persistence\"]],\"angular\":[[\"@tanstack/ai-persistence\"]],\"vanilla\":[[\"@tanstack/ai-persistence\"]],\"octane\":[[\"@tanstack/ai-persistence\"]]},\"mode\":\"install\"}"}},{"type":"component","name":"tabs","attributes":{"variant":"package-manager","mode":"local-install"},"children":[],"properties":{"data-package-manager-meta":"{\"packagesByFramework\":{\"react\":[[\"@tanstack/intent@latest\",\"install\"]],\"vue\":[[\"@tanstack/intent@latest\",\"install\"]],\"solid\":[[\"@tanstack/intent@latest\",\"install\"]],\"svelte\":[[\"@tanstack/intent@latest\",\"install\"]],\"preact\":[[\"@tanstack/intent@latest\",\"install\"]],\"angular\":[[\"@tanstack/intent@latest\",\"install\"]],\"vanilla\":[[\"@tanstack/intent@latest\",\"install\"]],\"octane\":[[\"@tanstack/intent@latest\",\"install\"]]},\"mode\":\"local-install\"}"}},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The second command wires this package's "},{"type":"link","href":"../getting-started/agent-skills","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Agent Skills"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\ninto your coding assistant. Run it before you start, because the recipes read your\nexisting database setup and write the adapter to match, and they encode the\ninvariants (full-overwrite "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"saveThread"},{"type":"text","value":", insert-if-absent run and interrupt\ncreates) that are easy to get wrong and expensive to debug."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"persist-state-on-the-server","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Persist state on the server"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Add the middleware to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" and point it at a backend. Here "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"persistence"},{"type":"text","value":" is a\nlocal "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"./persistence"},{"type":"text","value":" module: an adapter you build on the core over the database\nyou already run. "},{"type":"link","href":"./build-your-own-adapter","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Build your own adapter"}]},{"type":"text","value":" walks through\na complete SQLite version end to end."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  chat,\n  chatParamsFromRequestBody,\n  toServerSentEventsResponse,\n} from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { withPersistence } from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\nimport { persistence } from './persistence'\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const params = await chatParamsFromRequestBody(await request.json())\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n    messages: params.messages,\n    threadId: params.threadId,\n    runId: params.runId,\n    // Forward the resume batch so a thread with pending interrupts continues.\n    ...(params.resume ? { resume: params.resume } : {}),\n    middleware: [withPersistence(persistence)],\n  })\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream)\n}","lang":"ts","meta":"group=chat-persistence"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The middleware uses whichever "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"state"}]},{"type":"text","value":" stores the backend provides, no feature\nflags. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"messages"},{"type":"text","value":" is required; the rest are optional:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"messages"},{"type":"text","value":" (required) loads and saves the full model-message thread."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runs"},{"type":"text","value":" records running, interrupted, completed, failed, or aborted status."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interrupts"},{"type":"text","value":" records pending tool-approval / client-tool / generic waits, and\nrequires "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runs"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Need a mutex across workers? Add "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withLocks"},{"type":"text","value":" when other middleware needs\nmulti-instance coordination; see "},{"type":"link","href":"../advanced/locks","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Locks"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Creating tables on open is convenient for local development. In production, apply\nschema changes through your deployment workflow instead. See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./migrations","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Migrations"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"threads-runs-and-turns","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Threads, runs, and turns"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The transcript is stored per "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"threadId"},{"type":"text","value":", and each run gets a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runs"},{"type":"text","value":" record with its\nstatus, timings, and reported usage across provider calls. One thing follows from\nthat and matters when you wire a client: a reconnecting client never has to present\na run id it may no longer know. The store resolves the thread's live run with\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"findActiveRun(threadId)"},{"type":"text","value":" and the client tails that."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./id-map","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Id map"}]},{"type":"text","value":" covers how to choose a thread id and what both ids mean on the\ngeneration hooks. "},{"type":"link","href":"./internals","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How persistence works"}]},{"type":"text","value":" has the rest."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"subagent-cards-on-reload","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Subagent cards on reload"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When the run store keeps the child link, a refresh shows each subagent as a card."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Save these three fields on the child run. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createOrResume"},{"type":"text","value":" writes them on the first insert:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"parentRunId"},{"type":"text","value":": the chat run that started the child."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"subagentRunId"},{"type":"text","value":": the child run id."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"name"},{"type":"text","value":": the agent name."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reconstructChat"},{"type":"text","value":" calls "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"listByParentRun"},{"type":"text","value":". It puts one card on the parent assistant message for each child. Each card gets the child's full transcript back: text, reasoning, tool calls, and tool results. Nested children come back as nested cards."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A child that waits for an approval comes back with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"status: 'suspended'"},{"type":"text","value":", and its interrupt stays pending. The reloaded client can answer it, and the next run continues that child."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A child that a tool call started sits on the message with that tool call. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reconstructChat"},{"type":"text","value":" finds its parent run with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"listByThread"},{"type":"text","value":", so implement that method too."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Put "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":" on the parent "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" only, not on a child "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":". The parent stores the child runs. A child with its own "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":" stores the same child a second time, and its interrupt records conflict with the parent's."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Subagent support is optional. If the store omits "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"listByParentRun"},{"type":"text","value":", a reload shows the saved text and the cards stay absent. The conformance suite skips the subagent checks with no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"skipMethods"},{"type":"text","value":" entry."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The columns and the method are in the "},{"type":"link","href":"./store-reference","children":[{"type":"text","value":"store reference"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"ag-ui-activity-rows-on-reload","children":[{"type":"text","value":"AG-UI activity rows on reload"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An AG-UI "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ACTIVITY_SNAPSHOT"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ACTIVITY_DELTA"},{"type":"text","value":" event shows in the client as a\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"role: 'activity'"},{"type":"text","value":" row, for example a search progress card. The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"messages"},{"type":"text","value":"\nstore keeps only model messages, so a reload loses that row. Add an\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"activities"},{"type":"text","value":" store to keep it."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"loose":true,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Give the backend an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"activities"},{"type":"text","value":" store. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"memoryPersistence()"},{"type":"text","value":" already has\none. For your own backend, type it with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"defineActivityStore"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  composePersistence,\n  defineActivityStore,\n} from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\nimport type { ActivityRecord } from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\nimport { persistence as base } from './persistence'\n\nconst rows = new Map<string, Array<ActivityRecord>>()\n\nexport const persistence = composePersistence(base, {\n  overrides: {\n    activities: defineActivityStore({\n      loadActivities: async (threadId) => rows.get(threadId) ?? [],\n      // Full overwrite: `list` is the complete activity list for the thread.\n      saveActivities: async (threadId, list) => {\n        rows.set(threadId, list)\n      },\n    }),\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep your "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withPersistence(persistence)"},{"type":"text","value":" route and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reconstructChat"},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"GET"},{"type":"text","value":"\nendpoint. They save and restore the rows when "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stores.activities"},{"type":"text","value":" is set."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Render the rows on the client. Each one has one part with\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"type: 'activity'"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { fetchServerSentEvents, useChat } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\n\nfunction Chat({ threadId }: { threadId: string }) {\n  const { messages } = useChat({\n    threadId,\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n    persistence: true,\n  })\n  return (\n    <div>\n      {messages.map((message) =>\n        message.role === 'activity' ? (\n          <div key={message.id}>\n            {message.parts.map((part, index) =>\n              part.type === 'activity' ? (\n                <pre key={index}>\n                  {part.activityType}: {JSON.stringify(part.content)}\n                </pre>\n              ) : null,\n            )}\n          </div>\n        ) : (\n          <div key={message.id}>{message.role}</div>\n        ),\n      )}\n    </div>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The model never gets activity rows as input. A failed activity save does not\nfail the run. Each "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ActivityRecord"},{"type":"text","value":" keeps the row's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"metadata"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"keep-every-stored-message","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep every stored message"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":" merges incoming "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"messages"},{"type":"text","value":" into the stored thread by id."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"messages"},{"type":"text","value":" is empty, the middleware loads the stored transcript and the run\ncontinues from there."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"messages"},{"type":"text","value":" is not empty, the middleware merges by id:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The last incoming id that already exists in stored is a cutoff. Stored\nmessages after it are dropped. That is how reload removes the old assistant."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If no incoming id is in stored, every stored message stays (a new turn)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Same id in both lists: incoming wins."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"New ids and messages with no id are appended."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"saveThread"},{"type":"text","value":" replaces the stored thread with that merged list."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A client with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"history: { pageSize }"},{"type":"text","value":" posts only the new turn. Reload posts\nthe last user. Resume posts from that user through the painted assistant.\nSee "},{"type":"link","href":"./client-persistence","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client persistence"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"compaction-keeps-the-transcript-complete","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Compaction keeps the transcript complete"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Do you add "},{"type":"link","href":"../advanced/compaction","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withCompaction"}]},{"type":"text","value":" to the same "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":"? The\nsaved thread remains canonical. Compaction changes only the provider context,\nnot "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ctx.messages"},{"type":"text","value":". The message store keeps dropped content, summaries do not\nreplace old turns, and cleared tool output remains available for reloads."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If your adapter provides "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stores.metadata"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":" exposes it to\nother middleware. Compaction uses it automatically for validated checkpoints.\nSee\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../advanced/compaction#compaction-and-persistence","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Compaction and persistence"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"what-gets-persisted-and-when","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What gets persisted, and when"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":" writes at "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"four"}]},{"type":"text","value":" moments so a reload never loses a turn:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Moment"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What is written"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Best-effort?"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Start of a run"}]},{"type":"text","value":" ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onStart"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pending turn (just-submitted user message + prior history) so a reload mid-generation still shows the question"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Yes. Failure does not abort the run; finish is authoritative"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Interrupt boundary"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"New interrupt records, run status "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interrupted"},{"type":"text","value":", known usage, and a thread snapshot of current messages"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No. Store failures propagate"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Finish"}]},{"type":"text","value":" ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onFinish"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Complete transcript (including completed assistant messages, the terminal reply's stream "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"messageId"},{"type":"text","value":" for in-place reload identity, and any completed structured-output part), run status "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"completed"},{"type":"text","value":", known usage, and commit of consumed resumes"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No. The transcript is saved "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"before"}]},{"type":"text","value":" the run is marked completed"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Optionally while streaming"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Throttled partial assistant text when "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"snapshotStreaming: true"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Yes"}]}]]},{"type":"code","value":"const streamingMiddleware = [\n  withPersistence(persistence, { snapshotStreaming: true }),\n]","lang":"ts","meta":"group=chat-persistence"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Streaming snapshots default off (finish is the authoritative save); enable\nthem to trade extra writes for partial-output durability. Tune the interval\nwith "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"snapshotIntervalMs"},{"type":"text","value":" (default "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"1000"},{"type":"text","value":")."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The chat engine completes the canonical transcript before "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onFinish"},{"type":"text","value":" runs, and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":" saves that transcript directly."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Native-combined output keeps the structured result on its terminal assistant\nmessage."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Separate finalization can preserve a plain-text assistant message followed by\na structured-output assistant message."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Harness adapters emit "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"structured-output.complete"},{"type":"text","value":" during the run. A new\nmessage id stores prose and structured output as two assistant messages. The\nlast text message id keeps both on one assistant message."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A server-authoritative client hydrates that transcript on mount. Walk\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"messages[].parts"},{"type":"text","value":" for the reconstructed structured-output part:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { fetchServerSentEvents, useChat } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst PersonSchema = z.object({ name: z.string() })\n\nfunction PersistentStructuredChat({ threadId }: { threadId: string }) {\n  const { messages } = useChat({\n    threadId,\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n    persistence: true,\n    outputSchema: PersonSchema,\n  })\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      {messages.map((message) => {\n        const part = message.parts.find(\n          (candidate) => candidate.type === 'structured-output',\n        )\n        if (!part) return null\n        const person = part.data ?? part.partial\n        return <p key={message.id}>{person?.name}</p>\n      })}\n    </div>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx","meta":"group=chat-persistence"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The matching server "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"GET"},{"type":"text","value":" uses "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reconstructChat"},{"type":"text","value":". See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./client-persistence","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client persistence"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"error"}]},{"type":"text","value":", the run is marked "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"failed"},{"type":"text","value":". On "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"abort"}]},{"type":"text","value":", the run is marked\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"aborted"},{"type":"text","value":" with a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"finishedAt"},{"type":"text","value":"; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interrupted"},{"type":"text","value":" is written only at an interrupt\nboundary, and it is not terminal. Both terminal paths retain usage reported\nbefore the failure or abort. Resumes accepted in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onConfig"},{"type":"text","value":" are "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"not"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\nconsumed until a success boundary (interrupt or finish). If a run fails before\nthe resume commit, every pending interrupt stays retryable. The same is true\nwhen an atomic "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"commitBatch()"},{"type":"text","value":" fails: the whole batch stays pending."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The legacy sequential fallback is different. A write can fail after earlier\nentries were committed. Reload the thread's current pending interrupt records\nand submit only that remaining batch. Do not resend the original full batch."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"One abort does "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"not"}]},{"type":"text","value":" terminalize: a plain client disconnect on a run that some\nother middleware has declared "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"detachable"}]},{"type":"text","value":" (a durable event log plus a run\nstore, in practice "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withSandbox"},{"type":"text","value":" with durability wired). There, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onAbort"},{"type":"text","value":" writes\nnothing at all, the record stays "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'running'"},{"type":"text","value":", and the detach path records\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"detachedSince"},{"type":"text","value":" so a later request can take the run over. Intent is never\ninferred from the disconnect itself, because a user pressing Stop and a user\nclosing the tab produce the identical connection close; a cancel arrives out of\nband, either as the run's own abort reason or as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RunRecord.cancelRequested"},{"type":"text","value":", and\neither one makes the abort terminal again. See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../sandbox/takeover#detach-vs-cancel","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Takeover & Detached Runs"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The lifecycle a run record moves through. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"completed"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"failed"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"aborted"},{"type":"text","value":"\nare terminal; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interrupted"},{"type":"text","value":" is "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"parked"}]},{"type":"text","value":", not terminal. The normal client flow\nstarts a continuation with a fresh "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runId"},{"type":"text","value":". A server integration can reuse the\nsame "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runId"},{"type":"text","value":"; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createOrResume"},{"type":"text","value":" leaves its status "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interrupted"},{"type":"text","value":" until the next\ninterrupt or terminal boundary. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"findActiveRun"},{"type":"text","value":" only returns "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"running"},{"type":"text","value":" records,\nso it cannot discover a same-ID continuation while that continuation executes."}]},{"type":"code","value":"stateDiagram-v2\n    [*] --> running : run starts (idempotent createOrResume)\n    running --> completed : finish, transcript saved first\n    running --> failed : error\n    running --> aborted : abort (explicit cancel, or a non-detachable run)\n    running --> interrupted : interrupt boundary\n    running --> running : plain disconnect on a DETACHABLE run (detachedSince set, taken over later)\n    completed --> [*]\n    failed --> [*]\n    aborted --> [*]\n    interrupted --> [*] : continuation may use a new runId\n    interrupted --> interrupted : same runId pauses again\n    interrupted --> completed : same runId completes\n    interrupted --> failed : same runId fails\n    interrupted --> aborted : same runId aborts","lang":"mermaid"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"interrupts-survive-a-restart","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Interrupts survive a restart"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When a run pauses on an interrupt (a tool approval, a client-side tool, or a\ngeneric middleware request), the middleware records it. A later request on that\nthread must carry a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resume"},{"type":"text","value":" batch that answers the pending interrupts before\nnew input is accepted, otherwise it is rejected, which is why the example above\nforwards "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"params.resume"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For a mixed batch, the persistence middleware validates every entry before it\ncontinues. It commits all resolved and cancelled entries at one success\nboundary. An "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"InterruptStore"},{"type":"text","value":" can implement "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"commitBatch()"},{"type":"text","value":" to make that write\natomic. Without it, the compatibility fallback writes entries one at a time and\nis not atomic."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Persistence is the "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"server-authoritative resume path"}]},{"type":"text","value":": the middleware\nvalidates the resume batch against pending interrupts, builds\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChatResumeToolState"},{"type":"text","value":" (approvals / client-tool results / generic requests), and\n"},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"clears"}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"config.resume"},{"type":"text","value":" so the chat engine skips its ephemeral\nreconstruction (which needs client message history the persistence flow\ndeliberately omits). Resumes are committed (resolved/cancelled in the store)\nonly once the run reaches a successful interrupt or finish boundary."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onInterruptResolution"},{"type":"text","value":" still runs at the same moment as a request without\npersistence: after init "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onConfig"},{"type":"text","value":", before "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onStart"},{"type":"text","value":". See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../interrupts/apply-answers","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Apply Answers"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An interrupt record is born "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pending"},{"type":"text","value":" and only a commit moves it. With\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"commitBatch()"},{"type":"text","value":", a failed commit leaves the full batch answerable again. With\nthe sequential fallback, reload first because some earlier entries may already\nbe resolved or cancelled:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"stateDiagram-v2\n    [*] --> pending : run pauses, interrupt recorded\n    pending --> resolved : resume answers it, committed at a success boundary\n    pending --> cancelled : resume cancels it\n    resolved --> [*]\n    cancelled --> [*]","lang":"mermaid"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"where-to-go-next","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Where to go next"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bring durability to the browser too, so a full page reload restores the\nconversation and rejoins an in-flight run: "},{"type":"link","href":"./client-persistence","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client persistence"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Build the backend on the core, and look up the store contracts:\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./build-your-own-adapter","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Build your own adapter"}]},{"type":"text","value":". Whatever you already run\n(Drizzle, Prisma, Cloudflare D1, raw SQL), install the shipped\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../getting-started/agent-skills","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Agent Skills"}]},{"type":"text","value":" with\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"npx @tanstack/intent@latest install"},{"type":"text","value":" and have your assistant write the\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat-persistence.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" against your existing schema."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choose which stores to run: "},{"type":"link","href":"./controls","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Controls"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"persist-state-on-the-server","text":"Persist state on the server","level":2},{"id":"threads-runs-and-turns","text":"Threads, runs, and turns","level":2},{"id":"subagent-cards-on-reload","text":"Subagent cards on reload","level":2},{"id":"ag-ui-activity-rows-on-reload","text":"AG-UI activity rows on reload","level":2},{"id":"keep-every-stored-message","text":"Keep every stored message","level":2},{"id":"compaction-keeps-the-transcript-complete","text":"Compaction keeps the transcript complete","level":2},{"id":"what-gets-persisted-and-when","text":"What gets persisted, and when","level":2},{"id":"interrupts-survive-a-restart","text":"Interrupts survive a restart","level":2},{"id":"where-to-go-next","text":"Where to go next","level":2}]},"frameworks":["react","vue","solid","svelte","preact","angular","vanilla","octane"],"downloadMarkdown":"\nYou want a conversation to outlive a single request: the transcript, whether\neach run finished or is still waiting on an interrupt, all still there after the\nprocess restarts. `withPersistence` is a chat middleware that writes that\nstate to a store you choose, so the server owns an authoritative copy of every\nthread.\n\n<!-- ::start:tabs variant=\"package-manager\" mode=\"install\" -->\n\nreact: @tanstack/ai-persistence\nvue: @tanstack/ai-persistence\nsolid: @tanstack/ai-persistence\nsvelte: @tanstack/ai-persistence\npreact: @tanstack/ai-persistence\nangular: @tanstack/ai-persistence\nvanilla: @tanstack/ai-persistence\noctane: @tanstack/ai-persistence\n\n<!-- ::end:tabs -->\n\n<!-- ::start:tabs variant=\"package-manager\" mode=\"local-install\" -->\n\nreact: @tanstack/intent@latest install\nvue: @tanstack/intent@latest install\nsolid: @tanstack/intent@latest install\nsvelte: @tanstack/intent@latest install\npreact: @tanstack/intent@latest install\nangular: @tanstack/intent@latest install\nvanilla: @tanstack/intent@latest install\noctane: @tanstack/intent@latest install\n\n<!-- ::end:tabs -->\n\nThe second command wires this package's [Agent Skills](../getting-started/agent-skills)\ninto your coding assistant. Run it before you start, because the recipes read your\nexisting database setup and write the adapter to match, and they encode the\ninvariants (full-overwrite `saveThread`, insert-if-absent run and interrupt\ncreates) that are easy to get wrong and expensive to debug.\n\n## Persist state on the server\n\nAdd the middleware to `chat()` and point it at a backend. Here `persistence` is a\nlocal `./persistence` module: an adapter you build on the core over the database\nyou already run. [Build your own adapter](./build-your-own-adapter) walks through\na complete SQLite version end to end.\n\n```ts group=chat-persistence\nimport {\n  chat,\n  chatParamsFromRequestBody,\n  toServerSentEventsResponse,\n} from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { withPersistence } from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\nimport { persistence } from './persistence'\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const params = await chatParamsFromRequestBody(await request.json())\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n    messages: params.messages,\n    threadId: params.threadId,\n    runId: params.runId,\n    // Forward the resume batch so a thread with pending interrupts continues.\n    ...(params.resume ? { resume: params.resume } : {}),\n    middleware: [withPersistence(persistence)],\n  })\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream)\n}\n```\n\nThe middleware uses whichever **state** stores the backend provides, no feature\nflags. `messages` is required; the rest are optional:\n\n- `messages` (required) loads and saves the full model-message thread.\n- `runs` records running, interrupted, completed, failed, or aborted status.\n- `interrupts` records pending tool-approval / client-tool / generic waits, and\n  requires `runs`.\n\nNeed a mutex across workers? Add `withLocks` when other middleware needs\nmulti-instance coordination; see [Locks](../advanced/locks).\n\nCreating tables on open is convenient for local development. In production, apply\nschema changes through your deployment workflow instead. See\n[Migrations](./migrations).\n\n## Threads, runs, and turns\n\nThe transcript is stored per `threadId`, and each run gets a `runs` record with its\nstatus, timings, and reported usage across provider calls. One thing follows from\nthat and matters when you wire a client: a reconnecting client never has to present\na run id it may no longer know. The store resolves the thread's live run with\n`findActiveRun(threadId)` and the client tails that.\n\n[Id map](./id-map) covers how to choose a thread id and what both ids mean on the\ngeneration hooks. [How persistence works](./internals) has the rest.\n\n## Subagent cards on reload\n\nWhen the run store keeps the child link, a refresh shows each subagent as a card.\n\nSave these three fields on the child run. `createOrResume` writes them on the first insert:\n\n- `parentRunId`: the chat run that started the child.\n- `subagentRunId`: the child run id.\n- `name`: the agent name.\n\n`reconstructChat` calls `listByParentRun`. It puts one card on the parent assistant message for each child. Each card gets the child's full transcript back: text, reasoning, tool calls, and tool results. Nested children come back as nested cards.\n\nA child that waits for an approval comes back with `status: 'suspended'`, and its interrupt stays pending. The reloaded client can answer it, and the next run continues that child.\n\nA child that a tool call started sits on the message with that tool call. `reconstructChat` finds its parent run with `listByThread`, so implement that method too.\n\nPut `withPersistence` on the parent `chat()` only, not on a child `chat()`. The parent stores the child runs. A child with its own `withPersistence` stores the same child a second time, and its interrupt records conflict with the parent's.\n\nSubagent support is optional. If the store omits `listByParentRun`, a reload shows the saved text and the cards stay absent. The conformance suite skips the subagent checks with no `skipMethods` entry.\n\nThe columns and the method are in the [store reference](./store-reference).\n\n## AG-UI activity rows on reload\n\nAn AG-UI `ACTIVITY_SNAPSHOT` or `ACTIVITY_DELTA` event shows in the client as a\n`role: 'activity'` row, for example a search progress card. The `messages`\nstore keeps only model messages, so a reload loses that row. Add an\n`activities` store to keep it.\n\n1. Give the backend an `activities` store. `memoryPersistence()` already has\n   one. For your own backend, type it with `defineActivityStore`:\n\n   ```ts\n   import {\n     composePersistence,\n     defineActivityStore,\n   } from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\n   import type { ActivityRecord } from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\n   import { persistence as base } from './persistence'\n\n   const rows = new Map<string, Array<ActivityRecord>>()\n\n   export const persistence = composePersistence(base, {\n     overrides: {\n       activities: defineActivityStore({\n         loadActivities: async (threadId) => rows.get(threadId) ?? [],\n         // Full overwrite: `list` is the complete activity list for the thread.\n         saveActivities: async (threadId, list) => {\n           rows.set(threadId, list)\n         },\n       }),\n     },\n   })\n   ```\n\n2. Keep your `withPersistence(persistence)` route and `reconstructChat` `GET`\n   endpoint. They save and restore the rows when `stores.activities` is set.\n\n3. Render the rows on the client. Each one has one part with\n   `type: 'activity'`:\n\n   ```tsx\n   import { fetchServerSentEvents, useChat } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\n\n   function Chat({ threadId }: { threadId: string }) {\n     const { messages } = useChat({\n       threadId,\n       connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n       persistence: true,\n     })\n     return (\n       <div>\n         {messages.map((message) =>\n           message.role === 'activity' ? (\n             <div key={message.id}>\n               {message.parts.map((part, index) =>\n                 part.type === 'activity' ? (\n                   <pre key={index}>\n                     {part.activityType}: {JSON.stringify(part.content)}\n                   </pre>\n                 ) : null,\n               )}\n             </div>\n           ) : (\n             <div key={message.id}>{message.role}</div>\n           ),\n         )}\n       </div>\n     )\n   }\n   ```\n\nThe model never gets activity rows as input. A failed activity save does not\nfail the run. Each `ActivityRecord` keeps the row's `metadata`.\n\n## Keep every stored message\n\n`withPersistence` merges incoming `messages` into the stored thread by id.\n\nIf `messages` is empty, the middleware loads the stored transcript and the run\ncontinues from there.\n\nIf `messages` is not empty, the middleware merges by id:\n\n- The last incoming id that already exists in stored is a cutoff. Stored\n  messages after it are dropped. That is how reload removes the old assistant.\n- If no incoming id is in stored, every stored message stays (a new turn).\n- Same id in both lists: incoming wins.\n- New ids and messages with no id are appended.\n\n`saveThread` replaces the stored thread with that merged list.\n\nA client with `history: { pageSize }` posts only the new turn. Reload posts\nthe last user. Resume posts from that user through the painted assistant.\nSee [Client persistence](./client-persistence).\n\n## Compaction keeps the transcript complete\n\nDo you add [`withCompaction`](../advanced/compaction) to the same `chat()`? The\nsaved thread remains canonical. Compaction changes only the provider context,\nnot `ctx.messages`. The message store keeps dropped content, summaries do not\nreplace old turns, and cleared tool output remains available for reloads.\n\nIf your adapter provides `stores.metadata`, `withPersistence` exposes it to\nother middleware. Compaction uses it automatically for validated checkpoints.\nSee\n[Compaction and persistence](../advanced/compaction#compaction-and-persistence).\n\n## What gets persisted, and when\n\n`withPersistence` writes at **four** moments so a reload never loses a turn:\n\n| Moment | What is written | Best-effort? |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Start of a run** (`onStart`) | Pending turn (just-submitted user message + prior history) so a reload mid-generation still shows the question | Yes. Failure does not abort the run; finish is authoritative |\n| **Interrupt boundary** | New interrupt records, run status `interrupted`, known usage, and a thread snapshot of current messages | No. Store failures propagate |\n| **Finish** (`onFinish`) | Complete transcript (including completed assistant messages, the terminal reply's stream `messageId` for in-place reload identity, and any completed structured-output part), run status `completed`, known usage, and commit of consumed resumes | No. The transcript is saved **before** the run is marked completed |\n| **Optionally while streaming** | Throttled partial assistant text when `snapshotStreaming: true` | Yes |\n\n```ts group=chat-persistence\nconst streamingMiddleware = [\n  withPersistence(persistence, { snapshotStreaming: true }),\n]\n```\n\nStreaming snapshots default off (finish is the authoritative save); enable\nthem to trade extra writes for partial-output durability. Tune the interval\nwith `snapshotIntervalMs` (default `1000`).\n\nThe chat engine completes the canonical transcript before `onFinish` runs, and\n`withPersistence` saves that transcript directly.\n\n- Native-combined output keeps the structured result on its terminal assistant\n  message.\n- Separate finalization can preserve a plain-text assistant message followed by\n  a structured-output assistant message.\n- Harness adapters emit `structured-output.complete` during the run. A new\n  message id stores prose and structured output as two assistant messages. The\n  last text message id keeps both on one assistant message.\n\nA server-authoritative client hydrates that transcript on mount. Walk\n`messages[].parts` for the reconstructed structured-output part:\n\n```tsx group=chat-persistence\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents, useChat } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst PersonSchema = z.object({ name: z.string() })\n\nfunction PersistentStructuredChat({ threadId }: { threadId: string }) {\n  const { messages } = useChat({\n    threadId,\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n    persistence: true,\n    outputSchema: PersonSchema,\n  })\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      {messages.map((message) => {\n        const part = message.parts.find(\n          (candidate) => candidate.type === 'structured-output',\n        )\n        if (!part) return null\n        const person = part.data ?? part.partial\n        return <p key={message.id}>{person?.name}</p>\n      })}\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nThe matching server `GET` uses `reconstructChat`. See\n[Client persistence](./client-persistence).\n\nOn **error**, the run is marked `failed`. On **abort**, the run is marked\n`aborted` with a `finishedAt`; `interrupted` is written only at an interrupt\nboundary, and it is not terminal. Both terminal paths retain usage reported\nbefore the failure or abort. Resumes accepted in `onConfig` are **not**\nconsumed until a success boundary (interrupt or finish). If a run fails before\nthe resume commit, every pending interrupt stays retryable. The same is true\nwhen an atomic `commitBatch()` fails: the whole batch stays pending.\n\nThe legacy sequential fallback is different. A write can fail after earlier\nentries were committed. Reload the thread's current pending interrupt records\nand submit only that remaining batch. Do not resend the original full batch.\n\nOne abort does **not** terminalize: a plain client disconnect on a run that some\nother middleware has declared *detachable* (a durable event log plus a run\nstore, in practice `withSandbox` with durability wired). There, `onAbort` writes\nnothing at all, the record stays `'running'`, and the detach path records\n`detachedSince` so a later request can take the run over. Intent is never\ninferred from the disconnect itself, because a user pressing Stop and a user\nclosing the tab produce the identical connection close; a cancel arrives out of\nband, either as the run's own abort reason or as `RunRecord.cancelRequested`, and\neither one makes the abort terminal again. See\n[Takeover & Detached Runs](../sandbox/takeover#detach-vs-cancel).\n\nThe lifecycle a run record moves through. `completed`, `failed`, and `aborted`\nare terminal; `interrupted` is **parked**, not terminal. The normal client flow\nstarts a continuation with a fresh `runId`. A server integration can reuse the\nsame `runId`; `createOrResume` leaves its status `interrupted` until the next\ninterrupt or terminal boundary. `findActiveRun` only returns `running` records,\nso it cannot discover a same-ID continuation while that continuation executes.\n\n```mermaid\nstateDiagram-v2\n    [*] --> running : run starts (idempotent createOrResume)\n    running --> completed : finish, transcript saved first\n    running --> failed : error\n    running --> aborted : abort (explicit cancel, or a non-detachable run)\n    running --> interrupted : interrupt boundary\n    running --> running : plain disconnect on a DETACHABLE run (detachedSince set, taken over later)\n    completed --> [*]\n    failed --> [*]\n    aborted --> [*]\n    interrupted --> [*] : continuation may use a new runId\n    interrupted --> interrupted : same runId pauses again\n    interrupted --> completed : same runId completes\n    interrupted --> failed : same runId fails\n    interrupted --> aborted : same runId aborts\n```\n\n## Interrupts survive a restart\n\nWhen a run pauses on an interrupt (a tool approval, a client-side tool, or a\ngeneric middleware request), the middleware records it. A later request on that\nthread must carry a `resume` batch that answers the pending interrupts before\nnew input is accepted, otherwise it is rejected, which is why the example above\nforwards `params.resume`.\n\nFor a mixed batch, the persistence middleware validates every entry before it\ncontinues. It commits all resolved and cancelled entries at one success\nboundary. An `InterruptStore` can implement `commitBatch()` to make that write\natomic. Without it, the compatibility fallback writes entries one at a time and\nis not atomic.\n\nPersistence is the **server-authoritative resume path**: the middleware\nvalidates the resume batch against pending interrupts, builds\n`ChatResumeToolState` (approvals / client-tool results / generic requests), and\n**clears** `config.resume` so the chat engine skips its ephemeral\nreconstruction (which needs client message history the persistence flow\ndeliberately omits). Resumes are committed (resolved/cancelled in the store)\nonly once the run reaches a successful interrupt or finish boundary.\n\n`onInterruptResolution` still runs at the same moment as a request without\npersistence: after init `onConfig`, before `onStart`. See\n[Apply Answers](../interrupts/apply-answers).\n\nAn interrupt record is born `pending` and only a commit moves it. With\n`commitBatch()`, a failed commit leaves the full batch answerable again. With\nthe sequential fallback, reload first because some earlier entries may already\nbe resolved or cancelled:\n\n```mermaid\nstateDiagram-v2\n    [*] --> pending : run pauses, interrupt recorded\n    pending --> resolved : resume answers it, committed at a success boundary\n    pending --> cancelled : resume cancels it\n    resolved --> [*]\n    cancelled --> [*]\n```\n\n## Where to go next\n\n- Bring durability to the browser too, so a full page reload restores the\n  conversation and rejoins an in-flight run: [Client persistence](./client-persistence).\n- Build the backend on the core, and look up the store contracts:\n  [Build your own adapter](./build-your-own-adapter). Whatever you already run\n  (Drizzle, Prisma, Cloudflare D1, raw SQL), install the shipped\n  [Agent Skills](../getting-started/agent-skills) with\n  `npx @tanstack/intent@latest install` and have your assistant write the\n  `chat-persistence.ts` against your existing schema.\n- Choose which stores to run: [Controls](./controls).\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Chat Persistence\nid: chat-persistence\n---\n\nYou want a conversation to outlive a single request: the transcript, whether\neach run finished or is still waiting on an interrupt, all still there after the\nprocess restarts. `withPersistence` is a chat middleware that writes that\nstate to a store you choose, so the server owns an authoritative copy of every\nthread.\n\n<!-- ::start:tabs variant=\"package-manager\" mode=\"install\" -->\n\nreact: @tanstack/ai-persistence\nvue: @tanstack/ai-persistence\nsolid: @tanstack/ai-persistence\nsvelte: @tanstack/ai-persistence\npreact: @tanstack/ai-persistence\nangular: @tanstack/ai-persistence\nvanilla: @tanstack/ai-persistence\noctane: @tanstack/ai-persistence\n\n<!-- ::end:tabs -->\n\n<!-- ::start:tabs variant=\"package-manager\" mode=\"local-install\" -->\n\nreact: @tanstack/intent@latest install\nvue: @tanstack/intent@latest install\nsolid: @tanstack/intent@latest install\nsvelte: @tanstack/intent@latest install\npreact: @tanstack/intent@latest install\nangular: @tanstack/intent@latest install\nvanilla: @tanstack/intent@latest install\noctane: @tanstack/intent@latest install\n\n<!-- ::end:tabs -->\n\nThe second command wires this package's [Agent Skills](../getting-started/agent-skills)\ninto your coding assistant. Run it before you start, because the recipes read your\nexisting database setup and write the adapter to match, and they encode the\ninvariants (full-overwrite `saveThread`, insert-if-absent run and interrupt\ncreates) that are easy to get wrong and expensive to debug.\n\n## Persist state on the server\n\nAdd the middleware to `chat()` and point it at a backend. Here `persistence` is a\nlocal `./persistence` module: an adapter you build on the core over the database\nyou already run. [Build your own adapter](./build-your-own-adapter) walks through\na complete SQLite version end to end.\n\n```ts group=chat-persistence\nimport {\n  chat,\n  chatParamsFromRequestBody,\n  toServerSentEventsResponse,\n} from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { withPersistence } from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\nimport { persistence } from './persistence'\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const params = await chatParamsFromRequestBody(await request.json())\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n    messages: params.messages,\n    threadId: params.threadId,\n    runId: params.runId,\n    // Forward the resume batch so a thread with pending interrupts continues.\n    ...(params.resume ? { resume: params.resume } : {}),\n    middleware: [withPersistence(persistence)],\n  })\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream)\n}\n```\n\nThe middleware uses whichever **state** stores the backend provides, no feature\nflags. `messages` is required; the rest are optional:\n\n- `messages` (required) loads and saves the full model-message thread.\n- `runs` records running, interrupted, completed, failed, or aborted status.\n- `interrupts` records pending tool-approval / client-tool / generic waits, and\n  requires `runs`.\n\nNeed a mutex across workers? Add `withLocks` when other middleware needs\nmulti-instance coordination; see [Locks](../advanced/locks).\n\nCreating tables on open is convenient for local development. In production, apply\nschema changes through your deployment workflow instead. See\n[Migrations](./migrations).\n\n## Threads, runs, and turns\n\nThe transcript is stored per `threadId`, and each run gets a `runs` record with its\nstatus, timings, and reported usage across provider calls. One thing follows from\nthat and matters when you wire a client: a reconnecting client never has to present\na run id it may no longer know. The store resolves the thread's live run with\n`findActiveRun(threadId)` and the client tails that.\n\n[Id map](./id-map) covers how to choose a thread id and what both ids mean on the\ngeneration hooks. [How persistence works](./internals) has the rest.\n\n## Subagent cards on reload\n\nWhen the run store keeps the child link, a refresh shows each subagent as a card.\n\nSave these three fields on the child run. `createOrResume` writes them on the first insert:\n\n- `parentRunId`: the chat run that started the child.\n- `subagentRunId`: the child run id.\n- `name`: the agent name.\n\n`reconstructChat` calls `listByParentRun`. It puts one card on the parent assistant message for each child. Each card gets the child's full transcript back: text, reasoning, tool calls, and tool results. Nested children come back as nested cards.\n\nA child that waits for an approval comes back with `status: 'suspended'`, and its interrupt stays pending. The reloaded client can answer it, and the next run continues that child.\n\nA child that a tool call started sits on the message with that tool call. `reconstructChat` finds its parent run with `listByThread`, so implement that method too.\n\nPut `withPersistence` on the parent `chat()` only, not on a child `chat()`. The parent stores the child runs. A child with its own `withPersistence` stores the same child a second time, and its interrupt records conflict with the parent's.\n\nSubagent support is optional. If the store omits `listByParentRun`, a reload shows the saved text and the cards stay absent. The conformance suite skips the subagent checks with no `skipMethods` entry.\n\nThe columns and the method are in the [store reference](./store-reference).\n\n## AG-UI activity rows on reload\n\nAn AG-UI `ACTIVITY_SNAPSHOT` or `ACTIVITY_DELTA` event shows in the client as a\n`role: 'activity'` row, for example a search progress card. The `messages`\nstore keeps only model messages, so a reload loses that row. Add an\n`activities` store to keep it.\n\n1. Give the backend an `activities` store. `memoryPersistence()` already has\n   one. For your own backend, type it with `defineActivityStore`:\n\n   ```ts\n   import {\n     composePersistence,\n     defineActivityStore,\n   } from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\n   import type { ActivityRecord } from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\n   import { persistence as base } from './persistence'\n\n   const rows = new Map<string, Array<ActivityRecord>>()\n\n   export const persistence = composePersistence(base, {\n     overrides: {\n       activities: defineActivityStore({\n         loadActivities: async (threadId) => rows.get(threadId) ?? [],\n         // Full overwrite: `list` is the complete activity list for the thread.\n         saveActivities: async (threadId, list) => {\n           rows.set(threadId, list)\n         },\n       }),\n     },\n   })\n   ```\n\n2. Keep your `withPersistence(persistence)` route and `reconstructChat` `GET`\n   endpoint. They save and restore the rows when `stores.activities` is set.\n\n3. Render the rows on the client. Each one has one part with\n   `type: 'activity'`:\n\n   ```tsx\n   import { fetchServerSentEvents, useChat } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\n\n   function Chat({ threadId }: { threadId: string }) {\n     const { messages } = useChat({\n       threadId,\n       connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n       persistence: true,\n     })\n     return (\n       <div>\n         {messages.map((message) =>\n           message.role === 'activity' ? (\n             <div key={message.id}>\n               {message.parts.map((part, index) =>\n                 part.type === 'activity' ? (\n                   <pre key={index}>\n                     {part.activityType}: {JSON.stringify(part.content)}\n                   </pre>\n                 ) : null,\n               )}\n             </div>\n           ) : (\n             <div key={message.id}>{message.role}</div>\n           ),\n         )}\n       </div>\n     )\n   }\n   ```\n\nThe model never gets activity rows as input. A failed activity save does not\nfail the run. Each `ActivityRecord` keeps the row's `metadata`.\n\n## Keep every stored message\n\n`withPersistence` merges incoming `messages` into the stored thread by id.\n\nIf `messages` is empty, the middleware loads the stored transcript and the run\ncontinues from there.\n\nIf `messages` is not empty, the middleware merges by id:\n\n- The last incoming id that already exists in stored is a cutoff. Stored\n  messages after it are dropped. That is how reload removes the old assistant.\n- If no incoming id is in stored, every stored message stays (a new turn).\n- Same id in both lists: incoming wins.\n- New ids and messages with no id are appended.\n\n`saveThread` replaces the stored thread with that merged list.\n\nA client with `history: { pageSize }` posts only the new turn. Reload posts\nthe last user. Resume posts from that user through the painted assistant.\nSee [Client persistence](./client-persistence).\n\n## Compaction keeps the transcript complete\n\nDo you add [`withCompaction`](../advanced/compaction) to the same `chat()`? The\nsaved thread remains canonical. Compaction changes only the provider context,\nnot `ctx.messages`. The message store keeps dropped content, summaries do not\nreplace old turns, and cleared tool output remains available for reloads.\n\nIf your adapter provides `stores.metadata`, `withPersistence` exposes it to\nother middleware. Compaction uses it automatically for validated checkpoints.\nSee\n[Compaction and persistence](../advanced/compaction#compaction-and-persistence).\n\n## What gets persisted, and when\n\n`withPersistence` writes at **four** moments so a reload never loses a turn:\n\n| Moment | What is written | Best-effort? |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Start of a run** (`onStart`) | Pending turn (just-submitted user message + prior history) so a reload mid-generation still shows the question | Yes. Failure does not abort the run; finish is authoritative |\n| **Interrupt boundary** | New interrupt records, run status `interrupted`, known usage, and a thread snapshot of current messages | No. Store failures propagate |\n| **Finish** (`onFinish`) | Complete transcript (including completed assistant messages, the terminal reply's stream `messageId` for in-place reload identity, and any completed structured-output part), run status `completed`, known usage, and commit of consumed resumes | No. The transcript is saved **before** the run is marked completed |\n| **Optionally while streaming** | Throttled partial assistant text when `snapshotStreaming: true` | Yes |\n\n```ts group=chat-persistence\nconst streamingMiddleware = [\n  withPersistence(persistence, { snapshotStreaming: true }),\n]\n```\n\nStreaming snapshots default off (finish is the authoritative save); enable\nthem to trade extra writes for partial-output durability. Tune the interval\nwith `snapshotIntervalMs` (default `1000`).\n\nThe chat engine completes the canonical transcript before `onFinish` runs, and\n`withPersistence` saves that transcript directly.\n\n- Native-combined output keeps the structured result on its terminal assistant\n  message.\n- Separate finalization can preserve a plain-text assistant message followed by\n  a structured-output assistant message.\n- Harness adapters emit `structured-output.complete` during the run. A new\n  message id stores prose and structured output as two assistant messages. The\n  last text message id keeps both on one assistant message.\n\nA server-authoritative client hydrates that transcript on mount. Walk\n`messages[].parts` for the reconstructed structured-output part:\n\n```tsx group=chat-persistence\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents, useChat } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst PersonSchema = z.object({ name: z.string() })\n\nfunction PersistentStructuredChat({ threadId }: { threadId: string }) {\n  const { messages } = useChat({\n    threadId,\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n    persistence: true,\n    outputSchema: PersonSchema,\n  })\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      {messages.map((message) => {\n        const part = message.parts.find(\n          (candidate) => candidate.type === 'structured-output',\n        )\n        if (!part) return null\n        const person = part.data ?? part.partial\n        return <p key={message.id}>{person?.name}</p>\n      })}\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nThe matching server `GET` uses `reconstructChat`. See\n[Client persistence](./client-persistence).\n\nOn **error**, the run is marked `failed`. On **abort**, the run is marked\n`aborted` with a `finishedAt`; `interrupted` is written only at an interrupt\nboundary, and it is not terminal. Both terminal paths retain usage reported\nbefore the failure or abort. Resumes accepted in `onConfig` are **not**\nconsumed until a success boundary (interrupt or finish). If a run fails before\nthe resume commit, every pending interrupt stays retryable. The same is true\nwhen an atomic `commitBatch()` fails: the whole batch stays pending.\n\nThe legacy sequential fallback is different. A write can fail after earlier\nentries were committed. Reload the thread's current pending interrupt records\nand submit only that remaining batch. Do not resend the original full batch.\n\nOne abort does **not** terminalize: a plain client disconnect on a run that some\nother middleware has declared *detachable* (a durable event log plus a run\nstore, in practice `withSandbox` with durability wired). There, `onAbort` writes\nnothing at all, the record stays `'running'`, and the detach path records\n`detachedSince` so a later request can take the run over. Intent is never\ninferred from the disconnect itself, because a user pressing Stop and a user\nclosing the tab produce the identical connection close; a cancel arrives out of\nband, either as the run's own abort reason or as `RunRecord.cancelRequested`, and\neither one makes the abort terminal again. See\n[Takeover & Detached Runs](../sandbox/takeover#detach-vs-cancel).\n\nThe lifecycle a run record moves through. `completed`, `failed`, and `aborted`\nare terminal; `interrupted` is **parked**, not terminal. The normal client flow\nstarts a continuation with a fresh `runId`. A server integration can reuse the\nsame `runId`; `createOrResume` leaves its status `interrupted` until the next\ninterrupt or terminal boundary. `findActiveRun` only returns `running` records,\nso it cannot discover a same-ID continuation while that continuation executes.\n\n```mermaid\nstateDiagram-v2\n    [*] --> running : run starts (idempotent createOrResume)\n    running --> completed : finish, transcript saved first\n    running --> failed : error\n    running --> aborted : abort (explicit cancel, or a non-detachable run)\n    running --> interrupted : interrupt boundary\n    running --> running : plain disconnect on a DETACHABLE run (detachedSince set, taken over later)\n    completed --> [*]\n    failed --> [*]\n    aborted --> [*]\n    interrupted --> [*] : continuation may use a new runId\n    interrupted --> interrupted : same runId pauses again\n    interrupted --> completed : same runId completes\n    interrupted --> failed : same runId fails\n    interrupted --> aborted : same runId aborts\n```\n\n## Interrupts survive a restart\n\nWhen a run pauses on an interrupt (a tool approval, a client-side tool, or a\ngeneric middleware request), the middleware records it. A later request on that\nthread must carry a `resume` batch that answers the pending interrupts before\nnew input is accepted, otherwise it is rejected, which is why the example above\nforwards `params.resume`.\n\nFor a mixed batch, the persistence middleware validates every entry before it\ncontinues. It commits all resolved and cancelled entries at one success\nboundary. An `InterruptStore` can implement `commitBatch()` to make that write\natomic. Without it, the compatibility fallback writes entries one at a time and\nis not atomic.\n\nPersistence is the **server-authoritative resume path**: the middleware\nvalidates the resume batch against pending interrupts, builds\n`ChatResumeToolState` (approvals / client-tool results / generic requests), and\n**clears** `config.resume` so the chat engine skips its ephemeral\nreconstruction (which needs client message history the persistence flow\ndeliberately omits). Resumes are committed (resolved/cancelled in the store)\nonly once the run reaches a successful interrupt or finish boundary.\n\n`onInterruptResolution` still runs at the same moment as a request without\npersistence: after init `onConfig`, before `onStart`. See\n[Apply Answers](../interrupts/apply-answers).\n\nAn interrupt record is born `pending` and only a commit moves it. With\n`commitBatch()`, a failed commit leaves the full batch answerable again. With\nthe sequential fallback, reload first because some earlier entries may already\nbe resolved or cancelled:\n\n```mermaid\nstateDiagram-v2\n    [*] --> pending : run pauses, interrupt recorded\n    pending --> resolved : resume answers it, committed at a success boundary\n    pending --> cancelled : resume cancels it\n    resolved --> [*]\n    cancelled --> [*]\n```\n\n## Where to go next\n\n- Bring durability to the browser too, so a full page reload restores the\n  conversation and rejoins an in-flight run: [Client persistence](./client-persistence).\n- Build the backend on the core, and look up the store contracts:\n  [Build your own adapter](./build-your-own-adapter). Whatever you already run\n  (Drizzle, Prisma, Cloudflare D1, raw SQL), install the shipped\n  [Agent Skills](../getting-started/agent-skills) with\n  `npx @tanstack/intent@latest install` and have your assistant write the\n  `chat-persistence.ts` against your existing schema.\n- Choose which stores to run: [Controls](./controls).\n"}
